research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-344-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-345-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-346-**Given:** A compact set $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ and a real $\varepsilon > 0$. Throughout, $\theta := 2^{-1}$.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-347-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-348-[L1] $A$ is null when for every real $\eta > 0$ there are sequences $(a_k)$, $(b_k)$ with $a_k \le b_k$, $A \subseteq \bigcup_k[a_k,b_k]$ and $\sum_{k<i}(b_k - a_k) \le \eta$ for every $i$; $A$ has content zero when the same holds with a finite list ([[def-measure-zero-and-content-zero]]).
--
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-382-6.1 So for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ the finite list of step 4.1 covers $K$ with total length at most $\varepsilon$, which by [L1] is exactly the statement that $K$ has content zero; together with step 1.1 the two notions coincide on compact sets. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 4.1, step 5.1, L1] ∎
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-383-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-384-## Remarks
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-385-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-386-- **Compactness, not boundedness, is what does the work.** $\mathbb{Q} \cap [0,1]$ is bounded and null and does not have content zero ([[cex-null-set-not-of-content-zero]]); it fails to be closed, and the finite subcover step is exactly what it cannot supply.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-387-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-388-- **The theorem is what makes content zero usable at all.** Every set to which content zero is applied on this page is compact: the Cantor set ([[thm-cantor-set-properties]]) and the Smith-Volterra-Cantor set ([[def-fat-cantor-set]]) are both closed and bounded, so for them the two notions may be used interchangeably, and the finite form is the one that combines with [[lem-finite-interval-cover-total-length]].
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-389-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md:390:- **The cost of opening up the cover is $\varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1}$, chosen in advance.** Splitting the budget in half before the enlargement, rather than after, is what keeps the final total at $\varepsilon$ exactly; the same bookkeeping appears in [[lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null]] and in [[thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null]].
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/thm-compact-null-is-content-zero.md-391-````
--
