research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1633-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1634-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1635-**Given:** For $n \in \mathbb{N}$ let $P(n)$ be the assertion: for all reals $a \le b$ and all reals $c_0 \le d_0, \dots, c_n \le d_n$ with $[a,b] \subseteq \bigcup_{j \le n}[c_j,d_j]$, one has $\sum_{j \le n}(d_j - c_j) \ge b - a$. The lemma is that $P(n)$ holds for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
--
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1661-## Remarks
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1662-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1663-- **Why the argument does not simply take $[d_i, b]$.** The point $d_i$ itself may be covered by the deleted interval and by nothing else, so the remaining intervals need not cover $[d_i,b]$. They do cover $[d_i + \eta,\, b]$ for every positive $\eta$, and that is enough: the bound $b - d_i - \eta$ holds for all such $\eta$, and step 4.1 removes the $\eta$. Every attempt to shortcut this step by taking a closed left endpoint at $d_i$ is false as stated.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1664-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1665-- **Degenerate covering intervals are allowed and cost nothing.** A pair with $c_j = d_j$ contributes the single point $c_j$ and the length $0$, so a list may always be padded to a longer one, which is what [[lem-content-zero-implies-null]] does.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1666-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1667-- **The bound is sharp.** The single interval $[a,b]$ covers $[a,b]$ with total length exactly $b - a$, and no cover does better.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md-1668-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/ex-fat-cantor-measure-computed.md:1669:- **This is not the Heine-Borel theorem, and it does not use it.** The lemma is a statement about finitely many intervals and is proved by counting alone; compactness enters only when a countable cover has to be reduced to a finite one, which is what [[lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null]] does with it.
