research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1067-## Remarks
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1068-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1069-- **Two independent proofs of uncountability.** The route above is [[thm-perfect-set-uncountable-r]] applied to a nonempty perfect set. The other is claim 3 of [[thm-cantor-set-ternary-description]]: $C$ is in bijection with $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$, which is in bijection with the power set of $\mathbb{N}$, uncountable by [[thm-cantor-powerset]]. The two arguments share nothing, and the second is the one that makes the *size* of $C$ evident: $C$ is in bijection with the power set of $\mathbb{N}$, while having content zero. **It is deliberately not said here that $C$ has as many points as $\mathbb{R}$.** That would require a bijection between $\mathbb{R}$ and the power set of $\mathbb{N}$, and no such bijection is constructed anywhere at this point in the reading order; the two uncountability results available here are separate facts, one proved by the diagonal argument on power sets and one by nested intervals.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1070-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1071-- **Claim 2 and claim 4 together are the point of the whole construction.** A set of measure zero may be uncountable, so nullity is not a cardinality condition; and a nowhere dense set need not be null, so it is not a category condition either ([[fs-nowhere-dense-implies-measure-zero]], [[thm-fat-cantor-set-has-positive-measure]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1072-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md:1073:- **Why claim 5 is proved through measure and not by inspection.** The intervals making up $C_n$ have length $3^{-n}$, and one can see directly that a long interval cannot fit inside $C$. Doing that rigorously means keeping track of the $2^n$ component intervals of $C_n$ and their gaps; going through [[lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null]] uses the estimate already made in step 1.2 and needs no such bookkeeping.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1074-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure.md-1075-- **Every point of $C$ is a limit of other points of $C$, and the witnesses are explicit**: change one ternary digit far out, as step 2.1 does. This is also what shows $C$ has no isolated points without any appeal to the structure of its complement.
