research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1702-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1703-**Closed intervals lose nothing.** A bounded interval with endpoints
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1704-$a \le b$ is contained in $[a,b]$ and has the same length ([[def-interval]]), so
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1705-a cover by intervals of any of the four bounded forms yields a cover by closed
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1706-intervals with the same lengths. The definition is therefore stated with closed
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1707-intervals once and for all. Covers by *open* intervals are a genuinely different
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1708-demand, and passing to one costs a little extra length: the enlargement
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1709-$[a_k,b_k] \subseteq (a_k - \delta_k,\ b_k + \delta_k)$ is carried out where it is
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md:1710:needed, in [[lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null]] and in
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1711-[[thm-compact-null-is-content-zero]].
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1712-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1713-**Both notions are inherited by subsets.** If $B \subseteq A$ and $A$ is null,
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1714-then any cover of $A$ covers $B$, so $B$ is null; the same sentence with finite
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1715-covers shows a subset of a set of content zero has content zero.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1716-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1717-**A finite cover is a countable cover, so content zero implies measure zero.**
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length.prompt.md-1718-Padding the list $[a_0,b_0], \dots, [a_n,b_n]$ with the degenerate intervals
