research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2333-$a \le b$ is contained in $[a,b]$ and has the same length ([[def-interval]]), so
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2334-a cover by intervals of any of the four bounded forms yields a cover by closed
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2335-intervals with the same lengths. The definition is therefore stated with closed
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2336-intervals once and for all. Covers by *open* intervals are a genuinely different
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2337-demand, and passing to one costs a little extra length: the enlargement
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2338-$[a_k,b_k] \subseteq (a_k - \delta_k,\ b_k + \delta_k)$ is carried out where it is
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md:2339:needed, in [[lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null]] and in
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-cantor-set-properties.prompt.md-2340-[[thm-compact-null-is-content-zero]].
