research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305813- {"id":"thm-baire-category-r","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Baire_theorem","https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/06%3A_Differentiation_on_E_and_Other_Normed_Linear_Spaces/6.08%3A_Baire_Categories._More_on_Linear_Maps"],"rationale":"Both sources state Baire category for complete spaces and the dense-open-intersection form. The local statement specializes to the real line, adds the equivalent nonmeagerness clause, and makes the rational-endpoint construction canonical; its proof adapts the standard nested-set argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305814--{"id":"cor-q-is-meager-and-not-g-delta","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Book%3A_Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/06%3A_Differentiation_on_E_and_Other_Normed_Linear_Spaces/6.08%3A_Baire_Categories._More_on_Linear_Maps/6.8.E%3A_Problems_on_Baire_Categories_and_Linear_Maps","https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/06%3A_Differentiation_on_E_and_Other_Normed_Linear_Spaces/6.08%3A_Baire_Categories._More_on_Linear_Maps"],"rationale":"Zakon records that the rationals are meager and not G-delta and that a dense G-delta in a complete metric space is residual. The local item packages the complementary F-sigma/G-delta conclusions and supplies its own dependency-local proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305815--{"id":"def-measure-zero-and-content-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf","https://math.mit.edu/~brubaker/103PSet1Sol.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT materials use the same epsilon-small countable interval-cover definition of nullity and contrast finite covers for compact null sets. The local term content zero is a deliberately elementary finite-cover convention, so the package is a semantic restatement rather than an exact quotation of Jordan measure.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305816--{"id":"lem-finite-interval-cover-total-length","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/~brubaker/103PSet1Sol.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT solution states and proves that a finite interval cover of a nondegenerate closed interval has total length at least the interval length. The local proof changes the finite-overlap organization but follows the same covering estimate.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log:305817:-{"id":"lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/~brubaker/103PSet1Sol.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT solution gives exactly that a closed interval of positive length is not a measure-zero set and derives it from compactness and the finite-cover length bound. The local proof follows that argument with this library’s series indexing.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305818-+{"id":"cor-q-is-meager-and-not-g-delta","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/06%3A_Differentiation_on_E_and_Other_Normed_Linear_Spaces/6.08%3A_Baire_Categories._More_on_Linear_Maps/6.8.E%3A_Problems_on_Baire_Categories_and_Linear_Maps","https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/06%3A_Differentiation_on_E_and_Other_Normed_Linear_Spaces/6.08%3A_Baire_Categories._More_on_Linear_Maps"],"rationale":"Zakon records that the rationals are meager and not G-delta and that a dense G-delta in a complete metric space is residual. The local item packages the complementary F-sigma/G-delta conclusions and supplies its own dependency-local proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305819-+{"id":"def-measure-zero-and-content-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf","https://www.cs.uaf.edu/~maxwell/AY2007/math641/Measure.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT uses the epsilon-small countable interval-cover definition of nullity, while the UAF notes give the countable-cover formulation and the compact finite-subcover reduction. The local term content zero is a deliberately elementary finite-cover convention, so the package is a semantic restatement rather than an exact quotation of Jordan measure.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305820-+{"id":"lem-finite-interval-cover-total-length","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.cs.uaf.edu/~maxwell/AY2007/math641/Measure.pdf"],"rationale":"The UAF notes state the interval-cover length estimate used in the measure-zero argument and reduce compact interval covers to finite subcovers. The local proof changes the finite-overlap organization but proves the same covering estimate.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log:305821:+{"id":"lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.cs.uaf.edu/~maxwell/AY2007/math641/Measure.pdf"],"rationale":"The UAF notes give the countable interval-cover argument showing that an interval of positive length is not null, using compactness to pass to a finite subcover. The local proof follows that argument with this library’s series indexing.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305822- {"id":"lem-countable-sets-are-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT Homework 2.1 states exactly that every countable subset of Euclidean space has measure zero. The local proof is the standard geometric-budget cover, adapted to zero-based indexing and the library’s countability convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305823- {"id":"thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT Homework 2.1 states exactly that a countable union of measure-zero sets has measure zero. The local proof expands the standard double-indexed epsilon allocation and explicitly records countable choice.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305824- {"id":"lem-content-zero-implies-null","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"Finite covers are a special case of the countable interval covers in the MIT definition. The implication is a direct local verification from the two definitions; its proof is repository-local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305825-@@ -19,8 +19,8 @@
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305826- {"id":"rem-baire-in-r-is-choice-free","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baire_category_theorem"],"rationale":"The source distinguishes the ZF-provable separable complete case, including the real line, from the arbitrary complete-metric theorem equivalent to dependent choice. The local Remark narrows this to the canonical rational-endpoint proof and its dependency ledger.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305827- {"id":"fs-nowhere-dense-implies-measure-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.uchicago.edu/~may/REU2021/REUPapers/Jin%2CAlexa.pdf"],"rationale":"The fat Cantor source supplies the standard nowhere-dense positive-measure counterexample. The false claim and its local refutation are a dependency-level restatement of that counterexample.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305828- {"id":"fs-measure-zero-implies-nowhere-dense","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/06%3A_Differentiation_on_E_and_Other_Normed_Linear_Spaces/6.08%3A_Baire_Categories._More_on_Linear_Maps","https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources together record that the rationals are dense and countable, hence null but not nowhere dense. The local false statement is refuted by exactly that standard witness.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-305829--{"id":"fs-null-implies-content-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf","https://math.mit.edu/~brubaker/103PSet1Sol.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT sources distinguish countable null covers from finite compact covers; the local bounded-rational witness is the direct failure of the converse without compactness.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
